# Keep the Node scripts in scripts/ checked out with LF line endings on every platform.
#
# Why this exists: frontend/tests/integrity-log.test.js is the first test in this repo to
# `import` a scripts/*.mjs module into Vitest (the older script tests shell out to `node`
# as a subprocess instead, which is why nothing caught this before). Vite's transform
# rejects a CRLF .mjs with "SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token", even though Node
# itself parses the identical file without complaint. On Windows with core.autocrlf=true
# (the default on a fresh clone here) git writes CRLF into the working tree, so the suite
# would pass on Linux CI and fail on a Windows contributor's machine, for a reason that has
# nothing to do with the code under test.
#
# This is the same class of trap docs/adr/0003-client-integrity-and-anti-phishing.md already
# records for CSP hashing, where a Windows checkout's CRLF produced a hash no browser ever
# computes. Pinning eol=lf here fixes it at the source rather than making each consumer
# normalize.
*.mjs text eol=lf

# Every file type that ends up byte-for-byte in dist/ must also be LF everywhere, for a
# stronger reason than tooling convenience: the integrity manifest hashes the bytes that
# get served, and build-pages.mjs copies these straight through. With CRLF in a Windows
# working tree and LF on Linux CI, the same commit produces two different sets of hashes.
#
# That makes ADR-0003's central claim false. "Anyone can confirm wymber.app serves exactly
# the source in the repo" cannot survive the answer depending on which OS ran the build.
# Caught when frontend/tests/verify-tool.test.js passed locally and failed on CI with the
# same commit, on dist/robots.txt.
*.js   text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.txt  text eol=lf
*.svg  text eol=lf
*.webmanifest text eol=lf
